{
  "image_name": "1b9407f41380b4b692b9045af36aeefd.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how tissue changes from normal epithelium to an early lesion and then to aggressive cancer, alongside major shifts in immune signaling. In normal tissue, a single orderly epithelial layer sits on the basal membrane, and pathways involving ERVs, STING, NF-κB, interferon, and wild-type p53 appear balanced, supporting immune homeostasis.\n\nIn the early lesion, the epithelium becomes thicker and disorganized. Wild-type p53 is still present and is shown integrating oncogenic stress and DNA damage signals with inflammatory and antigen-presentation pathways. This stage seems to promote senescence, cytokine and chemokine release, PDL1 regulation, MHC-I, and other immune-related molecules, leading to recruitment or activation of CD8 T cells, NK cells, and M1 macrophages. Overall, this corresponds to an antitumoural immune response.\n\nIn aggressive cancer, the tissue is densely overgrown and highly disordered. The signaling box now centers on mutant p53, with disrupted links that appear to weaken immune surveillance. NF-κB-driven cytokines and chemokines remain, but the outcome shifts toward neutrophils, Treg cells, and M2 macrophages, with labels indicating reduced T-cell infiltration and IL-10-associated suppression. The overall message is that p53 status helps drive a transition from immune equilibrium, to early anti-tumour immunity, to an immunosuppressive tumour microenvironment.",
